\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {The full metric-class table, with the Spearman and within-correct columns.} Prediction-fit and weighted-similarity metrics rank on hard benchmarks and their saturated point estimates collapse or turn negative; raw overlap does not rank at all (its hard-benchmark inversion is a verbosity confound: controlling output length moves $-0.49$ to $-0.07$). Human columns are Pearson $r$ with bootstrap 95\% CIs and Spearman $\rho $ on the 60-item set; gold-token logprob uses the $n{=}42$ subset with logprob available; the within-correct column is from the canonical analysis (\texttt  {canonical\_validity.json}), with $n{=}48$ for every signal except gold-token logprob ($n{=}32$), and bootstrap CIs where computed (\texttt  {within\_correct\_cis.json}); the best value in each of the Pearson and Spearman columns is in bold. The free answer-correct bit row is from \texttt  {papers/analysis/free\_baseline.json}; it is constant within the correct-only subset, so its within-correct entry is undefined by construction, and its Spearman was not computed in that artifact. No signal in the table beats the free bit's $+0.92$: the prefix-repair curve mean's partial correlation with soundness given the bit is $-0.10$ $[-0.30,+0.22]$, while the bit's partial given the curve retains $+0.77$ $[+0.55,+0.95]$ (Section~\ref {sec:verify}). In rank terms the curve mean ($+0.41$) ties the referenced judge ($+0.43$) but trails the critic protocols ($+0.61$, $+0.54$) by $0.13$ to $0.20$. The within-correct column shows that no cheap signal, comparison or verification, demonstrably grades beyond correctness on this anchor set: every cheap signal's within-correct value sits inside wide overlapping intervals (\textsc  {TFA}{} $+0.21$ $[-0.10,+0.47]$), and the battery carries no multiplicity correction. The referenced judge's $-0.14$ $[-0.25,-0.09]$ reflects range restriction on the random set; the same judge reads $+0.81$ within-correct on a divergence-selected stratum of the companion audit~\cite  {companionA}, where selection inflates within-correct variance by construction, so its beyond-correctness grading is unresolved on the random set. \textbf  {Takeaway:} on the full set no comparison metric carries signal and the verification signals do, but the free answer-correct bit tops the field, and on the within-correct margin nothing cheap is separable from noise.}}{18}{table.caption.16}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:classfull}{{3}{18}{\textbf {The full metric-class table, with the Spearman and within-correct columns.} Prediction-fit and weighted-similarity metrics rank on hard benchmarks and their saturated point estimates collapse or turn negative; raw overlap does not rank at all (its hard-benchmark inversion is a verbosity confound: controlling output length moves $-0.49$ to $-0.07$). Human columns are Pearson $r$ with bootstrap 95\% CIs and Spearman $\rho $ on the 60-item set; gold-token logprob uses the $n{=}42$ subset with logprob available; the within-correct column is from the canonical analysis (\texttt {canonical\_validity.json}), with $n{=}48$ for every signal except gold-token logprob ($n{=}32$), and bootstrap CIs where computed (\texttt {within\_correct\_cis.json}); the best value in each of the Pearson and Spearman columns is in bold. The free answer-correct bit row is from \texttt {papers/analysis/free\_baseline.json}; it is constant within the correct-only subset, so its within-correct entry is undefined by construction, and its Spearman was not computed in that artifact. No signal in the table beats the free bit's $+0.92$: the prefix-repair curve mean's partial correlation with soundness given the bit is $-0.10$ $[-0.30,+0.22]$, while the bit's partial given the curve retains $+0.77$ $[+0.55,+0.95]$ (Section~\ref {sec:verify}). In rank terms the curve mean ($+0.41$) ties the referenced judge ($+0.43$) but trails the critic protocols ($+0.61$, $+0.54$) by $0.13$ to $0.20$. The within-correct column shows that no cheap signal, comparison or verification, demonstrably grades beyond correctness on this anchor set: every cheap signal's within-correct value sits inside wide overlapping intervals (\tfa {} $+0.21$ $[-0.10,+0.47]$), and the battery carries no multiplicity correction. The referenced judge's $-0.14$ $[-0.25,-0.09]$ reflects range restriction on the random set; the same judge reads $+0.81$ within-correct on a divergence-selected stratum of the companion audit~\cite {companionA}, where selection inflates within-correct variance by construction, so its beyond-correctness grading is unresolved on the random set. \textbf {Takeaway:} on the full set no comparison metric carries signal and the verification signals do, but the free answer-correct bit tops the field, and on the within-correct margin nothing cheap is separable from noise}{table.caption.16}{}}
